# Minecraft Plugin Development Rules for Cursor AI
# Developer: myztic_dev
# Target: Minecraft 1.21.4 with broad version compatibility

## Core Development Guidelines

### Plugin Structure & Standards
- Always use Maven or Gradle build systems (prefer Maven for simplicity)
- Follow standard Maven directory structure: src/main/java, src/main/resources
- Use semantic versioning (MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH) for plugin versions
- Include proper plugin.yml with all required fields
- Always include a comprehensive README.md

### Code Quality & Style
- Use Java 21 language features when possible, but maintain compatibility
- Follow Google Java Style Guide conventions
- Use meaningful variable and method names (no single letters except loops)
- Add comprehensive JavaDoc comments for public methods and classes
- Implement proper error handling with try-catch blocks
- Use logging instead of System.out.println (plugin.getLogger())

### Minecraft Version Compatibility
- Target Minecraft 1.21.4 as primary version
- Use Paper/Spigot API features appropriately
- Avoid version-specific code unless absolutely necessary
- When version-specific code is needed, use reflection or version checks
- Test compatibility with both Spigot and Paper servers

### Performance Best Practices
- Avoid sync operations on async threads and vice versa
- Use Bukkit.getScheduler() for proper thread management
- Implement efficient data structures (HashMap over ArrayList for lookups)
- Cache frequently accessed data
- Use bulk operations when possible (e.g., getOnlinePlayers())
- Minimize database queries and use connection pooling

### Security & Safety
- Validate all user input and command arguments
- Use parameterized queries for database operations
- Implement proper permission checks using Bukkit permissions
- Sanitize file paths to prevent directory traversal
- Never trust client-side data without validation

### API Integration
- Use Bukkit/Spigot/Paper APIs appropriately
- Implement proper event handling with @EventHandler
- Use Bukkit configuration API for config files
- Integrate with common plugins: Vault, PlaceholderAPI, WorldGuard when relevant
- Check for soft dependencies before using them

### Database & Storage
- Use SQLite for simple storage, MySQL/PostgreSQL for complex needs
- Always use HikariCP for connection pooling
- Implement proper database schema versioning
- Use async operations for all database queries
- Provide data migration tools for version updates

### Configuration Management
- Use YAML configuration files (config.yml)
- Implement configuration validation and default values
- Support hot-reloading of configs when possible
- Document all configuration options with comments
- Use nested configurations for complex settings

### Command System
- Implement proper command executors and tab completers
- Use CommandAPI or similar libraries for complex commands
- Provide helpful error messages and usage information
- Support both console and player command execution
- Implement proper permission checks for all commands

### Event Handling
- Use appropriate event priorities (NORMAL by default)
- Handle event cancellation properly
- Unregister events in onDisable()
- Use custom events for plugin-to-plugin communication
- Implement proper event listener cleanup

### Dependency Management
- Shade dependencies into the plugin JAR when needed
- Relocate shaded packages to avoid conflicts
- Use provided scope for Bukkit/Spigot API
- Minimize external dependencies
- Always specify exact dependency versions

### File Structure Template
```
plugin-name/
├── src/main/java/dev/myztic_dev/pluginname/
│   ├── PluginName.java (main class)
│   ├── commands/
│   ├── listeners/
│   ├── managers/
│   ├── utils/
│   └── config/
├── src/main/resources/
│   ├── plugin.yml
│   ├── config.yml
│   └── messages.yml
├── pom.xml or build.gradle
└── README.md
```

### Plugin.yml Template
```yaml
name: PluginName
version: ${project.version}
main: dev.myztic_dev.pluginname.PluginName
api-version: "1.21"
author: myztic_dev
description: Plugin description
website: https://github.com/myzticdev/plugin-name
depend: []
softdepend: [Vault, PlaceholderAPI]
commands:
  plugincommand:
    description: Main plugin command
    usage: /<command>
    permission: pluginname.use
permissions:
  pluginname.use:
    description: Basic plugin usage
    default: true
  pluginname.admin:
    description: Administrative permissions
    default: op
```

### Main Class Template Requirements
- Extend JavaPlugin
- Override onEnable() and onDisable() methods
- Initialize managers, listeners, and commands in onEnable()
- Clean up resources in onDisable()
- Implement proper shutdown handling
- Include version compatibility checks

### Error Handling Patterns
- Always catch and log exceptions appropriately
- Provide user-friendly error messages
- Implement graceful degradation when features fail
- Use plugin logger with appropriate log levels
- Include stack traces in debug mode only

### Testing Guidelines
- Write unit tests for utility methods
- Test with multiple Minecraft versions when possible
- Test with both online and offline mode servers
- Verify plugin works with minimal and maximum player counts
- Test all permission scenarios

### Documentation Requirements
- Include installation instructions in README
- Document all commands and permissions
- Provide configuration examples
- Include troubleshooting section
- Document API usage for other developers

### Deployment Considerations
- Build fat JARs with all dependencies included
- Test on clean server environments
- Provide clear versioning and changelog
- Include license information
- Consider publishing to Maven repositories

### Common Libraries to Consider
- HikariCP for database connection pooling
- Gson or Jackson for JSON handling
- Adventure API for modern text components
- ProtocolLib for packet manipulation (when needed)
- CommandAPI for advanced command handling

## Specific Instructions for Cursor AI

When generating Minecraft plugin code:
1. Always ask for clarification on plugin functionality before starting
2. Include proper error handling and logging in all generated code
3. Generate complete, working examples rather than code snippets
4. Include relevant imports and package declarations
5. Suggest appropriate Maven/Gradle dependencies
6. Consider performance implications of generated code
7. Include inline comments explaining complex logic
8. Validate that generated code follows these guidelines
9. Suggest testing approaches for the generated functionality
10. Recommend relevant Bukkit/Spigot API methods and best practices
